  "id": "Compound::DB01154 - Gene::2557",
  "verbalization": "Thiamylal, a thiamine antagonist, is shown to bind to multiple GABA-A receptor subunits, including Gabra6, Gabrq, and Gabra3, all of which are involved in GABA-A receptor activation. This binding is supported by a high confidence score (0.7722) in path_467, and similar scores in paths_468 and_469, indicating a consistent relationship across different subunits. The shared involvement of these subunits in GABA-A receptor activation suggests that Thiamylal may modulate GABAergic signaling through a common pathway. The mechanism of action appears to involve the inhibition of GABA-A receptor function, which could have implications for neurological and psychiatric disorders. The evidence is robust and consistent, supporting a direct relationship between Thiamylal and Gabra4 through the GABA-A receptor activation pathway.",
